      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one keep their current doctors if they join PACE?",
          "answer": "No. PACE requires participants to receive all medical care from the PACE interdisciplinary team. Your loved one cannot keep existing doctors, specialists, or other healthcare providers. The PACE team includes primary care physicians, specialists, nurses, and therapists who coordinate all care."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one lives outside the four PACE service areas?",
          "answer": "PACE is only available in Greater New Orleans, Baton Rouge, Lafayette, and Alexandria regions with specific zip code restrictions. If your loved one lives elsewhere in Louisiana, they cannot access PACE regardless of their medical needs or financial situation."
        },
        {
          "question": "How much will my loved one pay if they only have Medicare but not Medicaid?",
          "answer": "Medicare pays a portion of the monthly PACE rate, and your loved one pays the remainder. The exact amount varies by program, but private-pay participants typically pay $4,000–$5,000 per month for comprehensive services including medical care, prescriptions, and support services."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can I apply for PACE if my loved one has Medicare Advantage?",
          "answer": "No. Participants must have Original Medicare (Parts A and B) and cannot be enrolled in Medicare Advantage (Part C), Medicare prescription drug plans, or certain other programs. Your loved one would need to disenroll from Medicare Advantage during open enrollment before joining PACE."
        },
        {
          "question": "What exactly does 'nursing facility level of care' mean?",
          "answer": "Your loved one must need extensive daily assistance with Activities of Daily Living: bathing, grooming, toileting, walking, transferring from bed to chair, and eating. This is a clinical determination made through comprehensive assessment, having health conditions alone doesn't qualify if your loved one can manage daily activities independently."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one's condition gets worse and they need 24-hour care?",
          "answer": "PACE can provide increased home care services and skilled nursing visits. However, if your loved one requires 24/7 skilled nursing care or cannot safely remain in the community even with PACE support, they may need to transition to facility-based care and would no longer be eligible for PACE."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one apply for PACE while they're in the hospital?",
          "answer": "Yes. Hospital discharge planners can initiate the PACE assessment process. This is often ideal timing since hospital stays may indicate the level of medical complexity that PACE is designed to manage in the community setting."
        },
        {
          "question": "How quickly can PACE services start after approval?",
          "answer": "Processing timelines are not specified in available sources, but the assessment process involves multiple steps including LOCET screening, comprehensive iHC assessment, and PASRR screening. Contact your regional PACE office for current timeframes as this varies by location and individual circumstances."
        }
      ],
