      "intro": "If your parent is 65 or older, blind, or has a qualifying disability, and their income and savings are limited, Louisiana's Aged, Blind, and Disabled Medicaid program can cover doctor visits, hospital stays, prescription drugs, and potentially long-term care services. This is not the same Medicaid your parent may have heard about for younger adults. It is a separate pathway built specifically for seniors and people with disabilities, with its own income and resource rules.\n\nThere are two ways to qualify financially. The first is the standard (categorically needy) pathway, which requires income at or below 100% of the Federal Poverty Level. The second is the medically needy pathway, which helps people whose income is above the standard limit but who have high medical expenses. Louisiana also automatically extends Medicaid to people who receive SSI, though many SSI recipients still need to file a separate Medicaid application.\n\nThe program covers a broad set of medical services, and for seniors or adults with disabilities who need nursing home care, it can cover that too if your parent meets additional medical criteria. Resource limits apply: Louisiana uses a $2,000 limit for individuals under SSI-style rules. The home your parent lives in is generally not counted against that limit, but other savings and assets are. Understanding what counts and what does not is one of the most important steps before you apply.",
