      "contentSections": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "title": "Two income pathways: which one fits your parent?",
          "body": "Standard (categorically needy) pathway: income at or below 100% of the Federal Poverty Level. Your parent qualifies based on income alone if they also meet resource and other requirements.\n\nMedically needy pathway: for people whose income exceeds the standard limit but who have significant medical expenses. Louisiana applies the medically needy income level to this group. If your parent was told they earn too much, ask specifically about the medically needy pathway."
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "title": "What the $2,000 resource limit actually means",
          "body": "Louisiana applies SSI-style resource rules to ABD Medicaid. For an individual, countable resources cannot exceed $2,000. Countable resources include cash, bank account balances, stocks, bonds, and other liquid assets. The home your parent lives in is generally not counted. However, the full list of exempt assets was not published in the official materials available for this page. Before assuming an asset disqualifies your parent, ask the caseworker specifically whether it is countable or exempt. This question is worth asking before you spend down any savings."
        }
      ],
      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "My parent receives SSI. Do they still need to apply for Medicaid separately?",
          "answer": "Possibly yes. Louisiana says SSI recipients are automatically eligible for Medicaid, but the state also reports that many aged, blind, and disabled applicants must file a separate Medicaid application even if they receive SSI. Do not assume SSI equals active Medicaid enrollment. Call 1-888-342-6207 and ask a caseworker to confirm your parent's current Medicaid status before their next medical appointment."
        },
        {
          "question": "My parent's income is above the limit. Is there any other way they can qualify?",
          "answer": "Yes. Louisiana has a medically needy pathway for people age 65 or older, or disabled, whose income exceeds the standard 100% FPL threshold but who have significant medical expenses. Under this pathway, Louisiana applies the medically needy income level rather than the standard limit. If a caseworker tells you your parent earns too much, ask explicitly about the medically needy pathway before accepting that answer."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my parent keep their house if it is worth more than $2,000?",
          "answer": "The home your parent lives in is generally exempt from the resource calculation under SSI-style rules, which Louisiana uses for this program. The $2,000 individual resource limit applies to countable assets like bank accounts, stocks, and bonds, not to the primary residence. However, the complete list of exempt assets was not published in the official materials available here. Ask the caseworker to confirm which specific assets in your parent's situation are counted and which are not."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can a family member or adult child apply on behalf of an aging parent?",
          "answer": "Yes. You can apply on behalf of your parent if you have legal authority to do so, such as a power of attorney. You will need to bring documentation of that legal authority when you apply in person or include it with a mailed application. If you are applying online through the Louisiana Medicaid Self-Service Portal at medicaid.la.gov, call 1-888-342-6207 first to ask how to properly designate yourself as the applicant representative."
        },
        {
          "question": "Does this program cover nursing home care?",
          "answer": "It can, but not automatically. Your parent must meet two separate requirements: financial eligibility for ABD Medicaid, and the Nursing Facility Level of Care standard. The second is a clinical assessment of whether your parent needs daily help with things like bathing, dressing, or medication management. Ask the caseworker about both requirements when you apply, or ask the nursing facility's social worker to help coordinate the clinical review."
        },
        {
          "question": "How long will it take to get approved?",
          "answer": "Louisiana's official materials do not publish a single standard processing time for ABD Medicaid applications. Ask the caseworker for an estimated timeline when you submit the application. If your parent has an urgent medical need, tell the caseworker immediately. If your parent is in a hospital or nursing facility, apply now and let the facility's social worker know you are doing so. Some situations may allow for faster processing or retroactive coverage back to the month of application."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my parent apply for ABD Medicaid and the Medicare Savings Program at the same time?",
          "answer": "Yes, and if your parent has Medicare, you should. The Medicare Savings Program (a separate Louisiana program) can help pay Medicare premiums and cost-sharing for people who qualify. Applying for both at once through the Louisiana Medicaid enrollment line at 1-888-342-6207 is more efficient than filing separately. Tell the caseworker your parent has Medicare when you call."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens after my parent is enrolled and their income or health situation changes?",
          "answer": "Louisiana requires Medicaid recipients to report changes in income, resources, household composition, and living situation. If your parent's financial situation changes, contact 1-888-342-6207 to report it. Louisiana conducts periodic eligibility renewals, and missing a renewal notice or failing to return paperwork is a common reason people lose coverage unexpectedly. Watch your parent's mail for renewal forms and respond promptly."
        }
      ],
