      "intro": "If your aging parent struggles to get to the grocery store, stand long enough to cook, or simply eat consistently, Louisiana's Home-Delivered Meals program can send a hot, ready-to-eat meal to their home, typically five days a week. Meals are designed to provide at least one-third of the recommended daily nutritional requirements. There is no required payment for the meal, though some local providers encourage a voluntary donation.\n\nThe program is for adults 60 and older who are homebound or otherwise unable to prepare or obtain their own meals. There is no statewide income test and no asset limit. The practical question local providers ask is whether your parent genuinely cannot manage meals on their own, not how much money they have.\n\nThis program is run locally, not statewide. Each parish has its own Council on Aging or Area Agency on Aging that manages routes, schedules, and intake. That means the first step is finding the right office for your parent's parish, then calling them directly. What you get may differ by parish: some deliver hot meals five days a week, others may offer frozen or alternative formats depending on local capacity.",
